The '''Fascist Infiltration period (1917–1935)''' covers the subversion of Guajicutea's political institutions by fascist movements operating from [[Volisania]] and [[Sarta]], the erosion of democratic governance, and the country's transformation into a staging ground for the [[Great Adonian War]]. The period began with economic recovery under liberal governance but was progressively undermined by foreign-funded media manipulation, anti-[[Zong]] press campaigns, and the fascist takeover of the conservative party. The staged bombing at [[Waribá]] port in 1930 provided the pretext for a sweeping emergency package that suspended elections, banned opposition parties, abolished the anti-Volisanian laws of 1910, authorised a Volisanian military base, and created the [[Jefe de la Asamblea]] role that subverted the presidency. A second period of rapid industrialisation in the 1930s made Guajicutea a safe harbour for military manufacturing serving the Volisanian and Sartan war machines. The era ended in 1935 with the opening of the Balboa campaign of the Great Adonian War.

== Foreign influence and media manipulation ==

Two fascist movements projected influence into Guajicutea during this period, each through different channels. The [[National Volisan Party]] (NVP), which had been active in [[Volisania]] since 1911, sought to project influence into Guajicutea through military and economic means. The [[Sartan Fascist Party]] (SFP), which rose to power in [[Sarta]] in 1928, pursued influence through political meddling and the funding of sympathetic groups within Guajicutea.

The primary instrument of fascist influence in the media was the [[Círculo Volisano]], a Sartan cultural society that served as a front for Volisanian fascist operations. Technically a Sartan organisation, the Círculo Volisano sidestepped the 1910 ban on Volisanian ownership of private industries, which had targeted Volisanian entities specifically. In 1920, the Círculo Volisano purchased [[El Guajicuteano]], the largest newspaper in Guajicutea, through local intermediaries. Existing laws forbade foreign ownership of media, but enforcement was weak and the transaction was structured to obscure the foreign backing.

Under Círculo Volisano ownership, El Guajicuteano was transformed from a mainstream newspaper into a propaganda outlet. The paper ran a sustained anti-Zong campaign, publishing sensationalist stories about Zong violence, crime, and alleged espionage. The Zong population in Guajicutea was a very small minority, but El Guajicuteano and other newspapers that followed its lead portrayed the community as a violent threat. The campaign stoked public fears and created an atmosphere in which anti-Zong sentiment could be exploited for political ends.

== The Zong condemnation and the Escándalo de Balboa (1924) ==

The anti-Zong press campaign produced a diplomatic response from [[Zong]] in 1924. The Zong government sent a formal condemnation to Guajicutea protesting the treatment of Zong people within the country. The condemnation was a response to the media campaign run by El Guajicuteano and other outlets, which had been decrying the Zong population as violent and dangerous.

The liberal government attempted to prevent the situation from escalating, but it was struggling against the media campaign. The president undertook a diplomatic visit to [[Zong]] [[Balboa]] to repair relations after the condemnation. During the visit, a Zong man living in Guajicutea was accused of murdering his Guaji partner. The case was genuine domestic violence, not a political killing, but El Guajicuteano and the fascist press connected the two events: the president was portrayed as bowing to Zong while a Guaji woman was murdered by a Zong man. The visit was framed as subservience and weakness, and the president was accused of prioritising Zong relations over Guaji justice.

The scandal — known as the '''[[Escándalo de Balboa]]''' — discredited the liberal government and contributed to the conservative victory in the 1925 election.

== Religious revival ==

The 1920s saw a religious revival in Guajicutea, with two distinct currents producing communal conflicts. A revival of pre-colonial [[Guaji religion|Guaji spiritual practices]] gathered momentum, connected to the [[Bikuismo]] cultural movement and the rediscovery of the [[Catacumbas del Abismo|catacombs]] in 1892. Growing interest in traditional ceremonies, community rituals, and the three powers — sea, mountain, and sky — was seen by some as a cultural resistance movement against foreign influence, a return to Guaji roots.

A concurrent revival of [[Petran Catholic Church|Sartan Catholic]] religious practice was connected to the fascist movement's promotion of traditional Catholic values. The Sartan Catholic Church in Guajicutea was split between fascist-aligned clergy, who supported the conservative political movement and mobilised congregations around traditional values and anti-Zong sentiment, and anti-fascist clergy who opposed the political subversion of the church.

Tensions between the two religious communities produced conflicts over cultural policy, education, and the role of religion in public life. The fascist movement exploited these divisions, framing the Guaji revival as backward and the Sartan Catholic revival as modern and patriotic.

== Conservative government and fascist takeover ==

The Escándalo de Balboa discredited the liberals in the 1925 election. The [[Partido de Estabilización|PpE]], the largest conservative party, had grown by absorbing nativist sentiment during the depression and the anti-Zong campaign. The conservative government was led by a popular president, but the [[Asamblea Nacional]] remained split between multiple parties, making stable governance difficult.

From 1928, the [[Sartan Fascist Party|Sartan fascist government]] intensified its economic and political meddling in Guajicutea, providing funding and organisational support to fascist elements within the country. The [[Nuevo Partido de Aislamiento|NPdA]] (fascist) merged with the PpE. The PpE leader lost the leadership mandate to a fascist upstart who took control of the merged party. The takeover was supported by Sartan fascist groups and the Círculo Volisano. The merged party retained the PpE name but was effectively fascist-controlled. The fascist leader positioned himself as the defender of Guajicutea against the Zong threat, using the anti-Zong press campaign to build support.

== El Atentado de Waribá and the emergency package (1930) ==

In 1930, a bombing at [[Waribá]] port provided the pretext for the seizure of emergency powers. The bombing was staged by fascist agents within the PpE and blamed on Zong sympathisers. Several people were killed, and the fascist press immediately connected the bombing to alleged Zong military plans.

The fascist party leader used the crisis to force through a sweeping emergency package. The president, still nominally the conservative elected leader, was pressured into signing the emergency decree. The package included six measures:

# '''Emergency powers''' — executive authority to govern by decree.
# '''[[Jefe de la Asamblea]]''' — a new executive position that gave the fascist party leader control over the legislative agenda and, effectively, over the government. The president became a figurehead while the Jefe de la Asamblea ran the government. The role was justified as a temporary emergency measure but became permanent.
# '''Suspension of elections''' — citing national security concerns and fears of a Zong invasion.
# '''Abolition of the 1910 anti-Volisanian laws''' — the bans on Volisanian ownership of private industries and Volisanian language in commercial goods were repealed, opening Guajicutea to Volisanian economic and military presence.
# '''Volisanian military base''' — [[Volisania]] was permitted to establish a formal military base on Guajicutean soil, expanding beyond the existing port lease into a permanent installation.
# '''Ban on political parties''' — all opposition parties (PdI, PpLT, PdT, PSG) were banned. Only the PpE, now fascist-controlled, remained as the sole legal party.

== Protests and resistance (1930–1935) ==

The emergency measures triggered immediate local protests and civil disobedience. Riots broke out in Waribá, Tabakurí, and other cities. The government responded with police and military force to suppress the protests.

Organised resistance emerged under the banner of '''liberty movements'''. Some of these movements received covert funding from [[Zong]], which saw an opportunity to undermine Volisania's staging ground. Zong funding was channelled through intermediaries and Zong-connected trade networks. The fascist press used Zong-funded resistance as evidence of the Zong threat, reinforcing the justification for the emergency powers in a self-reinforcing cycle.

The ban on political parties drove opposition underground. Former party members, labour organisers, and civil society activists operated covertly. The regime responded with increasing repression through 1930–1935: kidnappings of journalists who criticised the government, arrests of politicians who protested the emergency measures, and suppression of political discontent. The regime's violence was political rather than racial — targeting dissent, not ethnicity.

== War production and industrialisation (1930s–1935) ==

The 1930s saw a second period of rapid industrialisation in Guajicutea, driven by military demand from [[Volisania]] and [[Sarta]]. Guajicutea was seen as a safe harbour for industrial manufacturing to support the Volisanian and Sartan militaries. Unlike [[Balboa]] and [[Tapuya]], which were clearly under Volisanian influence and vulnerable to attack or sabotage, Guajicutea was technically an independent state — not Volisania, not Sarta — making it a safer location for war production.

The ironworks in [[Waribá]], shut down since 1905, was reopened and modernised to support military production. Factories and manufacturing capacity expanded in Waribá and surrounding areas. Production focused on military supplies: processed materials, manufactured components, ship repair and maintenance at the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]], and other industrial outputs feeding the Volisanian and Sartan war machines.

Unemployment, still high from the depression era, dropped to near-zero as war production absorbed the available workforce. Sentiments were generally positive — employment restored, wages up, industry thriving — even as fascists ran the government. The economic recovery created a degree of public acquiescence to the regime. However, the industrialisation deepened Guajicutea's dependence on Volisania and Sarta: the factories existed to serve their militaries, not the Guajicutean domestic economy.

== Racial policy and the 1931 memorandum ==

The [[National Volisan Party|NVP]] in Volisania and the [[Sartan Fascist Party|SFP]] in Sarta were advocating for racial purity and ethnic cleansing in their home territories. However, the fascist leadership in Guajicutea took a different position. In 1931, a memorandum from the Guajicutean fascist leadership to Volisania advising against implementing racial laws in Guajicutea was leaked. The argument was pragmatic: Guajicutea's population was racially mixed — Guaji, Bayani, Kaftian, and Sartan — and imposing racial laws would trigger internal conflict and destabilise the war production effort on which Volisania depended.

The memorandum revealed the pragmatic streak of Guajicutea's fascists: they prioritised industrial output over ideological purity. The leak caused embarrassment but the policy held — Guajicutea did not implement racial laws during the fascist period. The regime's repression remained political rather than racial, targeting dissent and opposition rather than ethnicity.

== Cultural and social repression ==

The fascist regime repressed arts and homosexuality, framing both as degenerate. [[Bikuismo]] and independent artistic expression were suppressed. The cultural revival that had flourished since the 1890s rediscovery of the catacombs was stifled. The regime promoted state-approved art — militaristic, traditionalist, and propagandistic — and framed pre-colonial Guaji art as backward. Artists who continued producing independent work risked arrest.

Homosexuality was criminalised, with enforcement particularly strict within the armed forces. In Guaji history, homosexuality had been negatively regarded but never codified as illegal. It was understood as part of warrior naval culture — a tolerated practice among the maritime class, rooted in the pre-colonial tradition of men spending long periods at sea together. The fascist regime's repression targeted a practice that had been quietly accepted for centuries, framing it as degenerate and un-Guajicutean despite its deep roots in Guaji naval tradition.

== Guaji military buildup ==

The fascist regime built up Guajicutea's own military forces, which had been minimal since the republican era. The military was primarily used for domestic enforcement — suppressing protests, policing dissent, and enforcing the ban on political parties.

A specialised marine force, the '''[[Kari Buka]]''', was developed for amphibious assault. The name, meaning "sea openers" from ''kari'' (sea) and ''buka'' (to burst or open), connected the commandos to Guaji's deepest naval heritage and to Captain [[Kari Buka]] of the [[Kari Yabí]] voyage (1502–1514 CE). The amphibious assault specialty drew on Guaji's long naval tradition — pre-colonial canoe warfare, the Astillero Real shipbuilding heritage, and the island's maritime culture. The commandos trained for coastal raids, beach landings, and small-unit amphibious operations.

The Kari Buka was small but highly trained — a commando specialty rather than a mass army. Guajicutea's military was never large enough for conventional warfare, but the marine commandos represented a niche capability. Training was conducted with Volisanian and Sartan military advisors, integrating fascist military doctrine with Guaji naval traditions.

== Great Adonian War (1935) ==

By the mid-1930s, news and public consensus in Guajicutea pointed toward an approaching war. The military buildup, the industrial war production, and the geopolitical tensions between the Volisania-Sarta alliance and [[Zong]] made conflict appear inevitable. Most people were afraid, their sentiment a mix of economic satisfaction from employment and fear of the coming war. The fascist government used the fear of war to justify the continued emergency powers and the suspension of elections.

In 1935, the Balboa campaign of the [[Great Adonian War]] opened. Volisanian and Balboan forces invaded Zong Balboa. Guajicutea, with its Waribá port leased to Volisania and a Volisanian military base on its soil, was effectively a staging ground for the invasion. The era of fascist subversion ended with Guajicutea drawn into the Great Adonian War.

== Demographics ==

The population of Guajicutea grew from approximately 370,000 in 1917 to approximately 430,000 by 1935, a growth rate of approximately 0.8% per year, accelerating slightly with the war production boom of the 1930s.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Group !! c. 1917 !! % !! c. 1935 !! % !! Notes
|-
| Guaji || ~202,000 || 55% || ~232,000 || 54% || Natural increase. Stable share.
|-
| Bayani || ~53,000 || 14% || ~60,000 || 14% || Natural increase.
|-
| Sartan / Sartan-Guaji mixed || ~38,000 || 10% || ~45,000 || 10% || Increase from fascist political and military personnel.
|-
| Kaftian || ~55,000 || 15% || ~62,000 || 14% || Natural increase.
|-
| Other (incl. Zong) || ~18,000 || 5% || ~25,000 || 6% || Volisanian military personnel, Sartan advisors, Zong minority.
|-
| '''Total''' || '''~366,000''' || || '''~424,000''' || ||
|}

The Sartan share remained stable as political and military personnel from Sarta offset lower birth rates and some emigration. The "Other" category grew from 5% to 6%, reflecting the arrival of Volisanian military personnel and Sartan advisors during the fascist period.

=== Urbanisation ===

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Year !! Urban !! % !! Rural !! % !! Notes
|-
| 1917 || ~195,000 || 53% || ~175,000 || 47% ||
|-
| 1935 || ~245,000 || 58% || ~185,000 || 42% || War production accelerating urban growth.
